// `remove` is the array's word for both worlds, for the reason `push` and `insert` are: taking
// element i out is the same question of both. The answers differ -- a new array from the array, the
// removed element from the builder -- and that is the difference between the two worlds rather than
// between two names.
//
// Two consequences this pins. `remove(b, len(b) - 1)` is how `pop` is spelled, so discarding the
// element has to be legitimate; and it is, while discarding an array's `remove` is still an error,
// because that one copies and changes nothing. What keeps those two apart is `#[effectful]` on
// the builder declaration: `remove` is on the pure-builtin list, which is true of the array
// overload and false of this one, and the attribute is how the declaration says so. It was a
// callee rewrite onto `mutable_array_remove` until F208, and the only reason the pop form was
// accepted then is that the gate did not recognise the rewritten name -- an accident that read
// as a decision.
fn main() {
    var a = [1, 2, 3]
    a = remove(a, 0)
    print(a)

    val b = mutable([1, 2, 3])
    print(remove(b, 0))
    print(snapshot(b))

    // pop
    print(remove(b, len(b) - 1))
    print(snapshot(b))

    // discarding the element is how a pop that ignores the value reads
    remove(b, 0)
    print(len(b))
}
